WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.610
<v Instructor>So now let's talk about Amazon GuardDuty.</v>

2
00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.940
GuardDuty helps you do intelligent threat discovery

3
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.520
to protect your AWS accounts.

4
00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:09.600
How does it do it?

5
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:11.550
Well, it has machine learning algorithm,

6
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.050
performs anomaly detection,

7
00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.410
and uses third-party data to find these threats.

8
00:00:16.410 --> 00:00:18.210
So to enable it, it's just one click.

9
00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:19.800
Then you have 30 days trial.

10
00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.990
You don't need to install any software.

11
00:00:21.990 --> 00:00:24.540
So GuardDuty is going to look at a lot of input data,

12
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.340
such as your CloudTrail event logs

13
00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:28.500
to look for unusual API calls

14
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.180
or unauthorized deployments.

15
00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.460
It's going to look at your management events

16
00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:33.990
and your data events,

17
00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.270
so for example, on the management side,

18
00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:39.000
the create VPC subnet event, and so on

19
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.010
Whereas, on the S3 data events,

20
00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:43.290
for example, get objects, list objects,

21
00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:45.030
delete objects, and so on.

22
00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.100
And then for VPC Flow Logs, it's going to look

23
00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.960
at unusual internet traffic.

24
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.590
It's going to look at unusual IP addresses.

25
00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:56.580
DNS logs to look at EC2 instances sending encoded data

26
00:00:56.580 --> 00:01:00.090
within DNS queries, which would mean they're compromised,

27
00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:02.805
and optional features to allow you to analyze, for example,

28
00:01:02.805 --> 00:01:06.090
other input data sources, such as your EKS audit logs,

29
00:01:06.090 --> 00:01:08.670
your RDS and Aurora login events,

30
00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:13.560
your EBS, your Lambda, and your S3 data events.

31
00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.470
So we can also set up EventBridge rules

32
00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:19.530
to be notified automatically in case you have findings.

33
00:01:19.530 --> 00:01:20.700
And then these rules can target

34
00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.710
whatever EventBridge can target,

35
00:01:22.710 --> 00:01:26.250
such as AWS Lambda or SNS topics.

36
00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:28.470
Also, this can come up in the exam.

37
00:01:28.470 --> 00:01:31.350
GuardDuty is a very good tool to protect you

38
00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:33.210
against cryptocurrency attacks

39
00:01:33.210 --> 00:01:35.190
because there is a dedicated finding for it,

40
00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:37.860
so it knows how to analyze all these input data

41
00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.070
and find that there is a cryptocurrency attack.

42
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.110
So to summarize, within GuardDuty,

43
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:45.000
we have several input data.

44
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.530
We have the VPC Flow Logs,

45
00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.080
the CloudTrail logs, and the DNS logs

46
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:51.300
that will be no matter what into GuardDuty,

47
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.760
as well as some optional features you can enable,

48
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.830
such as your S3 logs, your EBS volumes,

49
00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:59.640
your Lambda network activity, RDS and Aurora login activity,

50
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:02.370
and your EKS audit logs and runtime monitoring

51
00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.460
as well as most likely more features over time

52
00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.660
that I will not put here

53
00:02:06.660 --> 00:02:09.180
because you get the idea of optional features.

54
00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.190
And so from all these things,

55
00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:13.230
then GuardDuty can generate findings,

56
00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:15.300
and if these findings are detected,

57
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.750
an event is created in Amazon EventBridge.

58
00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:20.850
And therefore, from Event Bridge, thanks to rules,

59
00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:22.110
we can trigger automations,

60
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:23.850
for example, using Lambda functions,

61
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:27.630
or send notifications, for example, using SNS.

62
00:02:27.630 --> 00:02:28.800
You need to know another feature

63
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:31.890
of GuardDuty called the Delegated Administrator.

64
00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:34.530
So when you have an AWS Organization,

65
00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.230
one of the member accounts can be designated

66
00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.870
a GuardDuty Delegated Administrator.

67
00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:42.750
The idea is that that one account has full permission

68
00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.540
to enable and manage GuardDuty for all accounts

69
00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.070
in the Organization.

70
00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:48.570
So you have your Management accounts,

71
00:02:48.570 --> 00:02:50.250
and you want to have GuardDuty enabled

72
00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:52.470
for several member accounts.

73
00:02:52.470 --> 00:02:55.620
Therefore, you're going to name one of these member accounts

74
00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:58.140
a Delegated Administrator for GuardDuty,

75
00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.140
and it will have the power to enable and manage GuardDuty

76
00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.230
on all the accounts within the Organization.

77
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.200
Now to name a Delegated Administrator,

78
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.730
this can only be done using

79
00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:11.880
the Organization Management Account.

80
00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:13.770
Okay, so that's it for GuardDuty.

81
00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.770
I hope you liked it, and I will see you in the next lecture.

